# Mermaid environment — copy to .env and fill in. NEVER commit .env.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ── PostgreSQL: TWO ROLES, and the distinction is not optional ──────────────────────────────
#
# Managed hosts hand you an owner role, and on several of them (Neon's neondb_owner, Supabase's
# postgres) that role carries BYPASSRLS — meaning it IGNORES row-level security entirely. Migration
# 0002 FORCEs RLS, which covers a plain table owner, but nothing can make a BYPASSRLS or SUPERUSER
# role obey a policy. Run the app as one and tenant isolation is decorative.
#
# So:
#   DATABASE_ADMIN_URL → the owner/admin. Runs migrations (DDL). The app must NEVER hold it.
#   DATABASE_URL       → an unprivileged role (NOSUPERUSER, NOBYPASSRLS, DML only). What the app uses.
#
# `npm run db:provision-role` creates that role for you from the admin URL and prints its password:
#
#   DATABASE_ADMIN_URL=postgres://owner:...@host/db npm run db:provision-role
#
# `npm run db:verify` refuses to run under a role that can bypass RLS, so a misconfiguration fails
# with an explanation rather than a misleading "RLS LEAK".
DATABASE_URL=postgres://mermaid_app:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/mermaid?sslmode=require
DATABASE_ADMIN_URL=postgres://OWNER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/mermaid?sslmode=require

# Optional: name of the application role provisioned above (default: mermaid_app), and an explicit
# password for automated environments (CI needs to know it in advance to build DATABASE_URL).
# APP_DB_ROLE=mermaid_app
# APP_DB_PASSWORD=

# ── TLS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every connection this repository opens — the pool AND the operational scripts — pins verified TLS,
# regardless of what `sslmode` in the URL above says. `sslmode=no-verify` cannot weaken it.
#
# The ONE relaxation is for a local container that speaks no TLS (docker-compose, CI's service
# container), and it takes two keys together: this variable AND a loopback LITERAL in the host. So
# leaving it set in your shell cannot downgrade a connection to a managed database — that one still
# gets verified TLS. Leave it unset for anything remote.
# PGSSLMODE=disable

# Runtime
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3000

# ── The practice's timezone ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Timely-filing and appeal deadlines are CALENDAR DAYS in the clinic's own day, not the server's.
# Left unset the engines count in UTC, and for a US practice that reports claims expired from late
# afternoon on the last day they were filable — the hours when end-of-day filing sweeps run. Staff
# are told to abandon revenue the payer would still have paid, and nothing on screen says the date
# is a guess.
#
# Must be an IANA REGION name. The legacy abbreviations (EST, PST) are refused: they are fixed
# offsets that ignore daylight saving, so they drift by an hour for most of the year.
#
# Production refuses to start without it, for the same reason it refuses the demo billing identity.
# PRACTICE_TIMEZONE=America/Los_Angeles

# ── PHI at rest ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Three columns hold raw protected health information exactly as the outside world sent it: the card
# text read off a patient's insurance card, the payer's 271, and the 837 filed on their behalf. They
# are kept because provenance is the whole reason to keep them — and they are the highest-value
# target in the database.
#
# Set this and they are AES-256-GCM encrypted before they reach Postgres. Deliberately application
# level rather than pgcrypto: pgp_sym_encrypt() would make the key a query parameter, which puts it
# in pg_stat_statements and in every slow-query log.
#
# Generate one:
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# Leave it unset in development and the columns are written in the clear (the laptop demo needs no
# key). With NODE_ENV=production the app REFUSES TO START without it — a warning in a log nobody
# reads is how a compliance control becomes a compliance finding.
#
# Rows written before the key existed keep reading: `decryptPhi` passes through anything that is not
# already an envelope, so this ships without a maintenance window. Backfill the old rows whenever it
# suits, with `npm run db:encrypt-phi`.
#
# LOSING THIS KEY MEANS LOSING THE DATA IT PROTECTS. Store it wherever you store database passwords.
# PHI_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Adapters (wired in later phases) ---
# EDI clearinghouse. 'mock' uses the ported X12 codecs and is the laptop-demo default.
# 'stedi' sends real 270s to Stedi and parses the real 271 that comes back.
#
# ⚠ EDI_ADAPTER=stedi currently serves ELIGIBILITY ONLY. submitClaim / fetchRemittance /
#   parseRemittance refuse with a permanent (non-retryable) error — filing a claim or posting money
#   through an unbuilt vendor path is not something to do by accident. Keep 'mock' for those.
EDI_ADAPTER=mock

# Stedi. Test keys are prefixed `test_`; the adapter REFUSES to start if the prefix and
# STEDI_TEST_MODE disagree — a production key in test mode reaches real payers with real member IDs,
# and a test key in production mode makes a clinic quote patients from mock benefits that look real.
# STEDI_API_KEY=
# Opt IN to production. Unset or 1 = test. Only `0` reaches a real payer.
# STEDI_TEST_MODE=1
# STEDI_BASE_URL=https://healthcare.us.stedi.com
# Stedi's EDI-PLATFORM host — a different service from the healthcare host above. ERA polling and
# artifact retrieval live here, and probing the healthcare host for those paths is what once
# produced the (wrong) conclusion that Stedi had no polling endpoint at all.
# STEDI_CORE_BASE_URL=https://core.us.stedi.com
# Extra hosts an ERA artifact may be downloaded from, comma-separated. The second hop of an ERA
# retrieval is an UNAUTHENTICATED GET of a URL the vendor's response supplies, so the host is checked
# against an allowlist rather than trusted. Additive to the built-in defaults (stedi.com,
# amazonaws.com, cloudfront.net) — it cannot remove them.
# STEDI_ARTIFACT_HOSTS=
# STEDI_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# LLM provider: 'off' uses deterministic engines only, and is a fully supported state — every AI
# feature degrades to its deterministic path and the revenue cycle is unaffected.
# Registered: 'off' | 'anthropic'. An unknown name degrades to 'off' with a process warning rather
# than refusing to boot: unlike a missing EDI vendor, a missing LLM only costs prose on a screen.
LLM_PROVIDER=off
# LLM_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# LLM_API_KEY=
# LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
# LLM_MAX_ATTEMPTS=2
#
# LLM_PHI_APPROVED — the one compliance control a production deployment must switch ON rather than
# configure correctly. Set it to 1 ONLY once a BAA covering the exact configured endpoint is signed.
# Until then every patient-bearing AI call is refused before it leaves the process (it is refused,
# not redacted — a half-redacted prompt is still a disclosure and also a worse answer), and the
# deterministic paths carry the work. Forgetting this costs a convenience; defaulting it the other
# way costs an unreported disclosure of PHI. See platform/llm/src/phi.ts.
# LLM_PHI_APPROVED=0

# --- Auth ---
SESSION_TTL_HOURS=12
# Automatic logoff (45 CFR 164.312(a)(2)(iii)): minutes a signed-in screen may sit untouched before
# its session ends and the browser locks. 1–720, default 30; anything else refuses to boot. Background
# polling does not count as activity. Apply migration 0036 BEFORE deploying the code that reads it.
# SESSION_IDLE_MINUTES=30
SECURE_COOKIES=0
# WHERE the login rate limiter and the audit trail get a client's address. Three answers, and
# production refuses to boot without one:
#
#   TRUST_PROXY=cloudflare   Read cf-connecting-ip — the value Cloudflare's edge generates from the
#                            connection it terminated. Correct for every *.onrender.com host and for
#                            any custom domain proxied through Cloudflare. Trustworthy exactly while
#                            nothing can reach this process AROUND that edge.
#   TRUST_PROXY=<n>          n trusted proxies, each appending the peer it saw to x-forwarded-for;
#                            the nth entry FROM THE RIGHT is the client. 1 = a single balancer.
#                            2 = Cloudflare in front of Render. Count it wrong and the throttle
#                            silently stops working, so prefer `cloudflare` where it applies.
#   TRUST_PROXY=0            Nothing trusted in front; the socket's peer address is the client.
#
# Never the FIRST x-forwarded-for entry: Cloudflare and every other proxy APPEND, so the left of
# that chain is whatever the client typed, and a password-guessing script that varies it would
# rotate its own rate-limit identity.
#
# Why this is not a boolean any more: with TRUST_PROXY=1 behind
# Cloudflare-in-front-of-Render, the last entry was a rotating Cloudflare edge node, so twenty
# parallel failed logins gave nineteen 401s and one 429 where a capacity-10 bucket should give about
# ten of each. With 0, every audit row recorded ip=::1, because Render's router reaches the process
# over loopback.
# TRUST_PROXY=cloudflare
#
# HOSTED=1 forces the hosted-platform detection on for a platform the built-in list does not
# recognise. That detection drives secure cookies and password-reset token withholding; it
# deliberately does NOT drive TRUST_PROXY, for the reason above. Being hosted does NOT imply
# NODE_ENV=production — see DEPLOYING.md.
# HOSTED=1
# AUTH_SEED_* passwords for demo accounts (dev only)

# --- Operational alerting ---
# Where an alert GOES, as opposed to where it is decided.
#
# The alert gate (platform/observability/src/alerts.ts) is careful: a condition must hold for three
# consecutive checks before anybody is told, repeats are capped at one per fifteen minutes, and
# "it cleared" is announced once because that is what tells an operator to stop looking. All of that
# was writing to stdout and nowhere else, which on a container whose logs are discarded on restart is
# the same as no alerting at all — and the two things it watches are the two this platform is least
# able to notice on its own: a stalled outbox (the learning loop silently stops) and money that will
# not reconcile against the payer's own 835.
#
# One JSON POST, which is what Slack, Discord, PagerDuty Events v2, Opsgenie and a fifteen-line
# script on your own server all accept. The body leads with a `text` field so the commonest
# destinations render it with no mapping at all.
#
# https ONLY. An http:// URL is REFUSED rather than used: an alert body names this deployment and
# says which practice's money is in question, and the mistake would be invisible because the alert
# still arrives. Unset is a supported state and changes nothing.
# ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...
#
# Carried on every alert so two environments posting into one channel are tellable apart. Defaults
# to NODE_ENV, which is honest and usually enough.
# DEPLOYMENT_NAME=staging

# --- Billing provider (837D loop 2010AA) ---
# Stamped onto every outbound claim. Pilot scope is one practice, so it lives here; in multi-practice
# production these come from the `practices` row for the authenticated session, never from config.
# The claim scrubber validates them: an NPI failing its check digit BLOCKS filing, and the
# placeholder tax ID below raises a warning on every claim until you replace it.
BILLING_PROVIDER_NAME=MERMAID DEMO DENTAL
BILLING_PROVIDER_NPI=1234567893
BILLING_PROVIDER_TAX_ID=000000000

# ── REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION, AND UNDOCUMENTED UNTIL NOW (NEW-19) ────────────────────────────────
# These five were read by `getConfig()`, enforced by `assertBillingProviderContactConfigured` — a
# production process REFUSES TO START without them — and named in no example file. So the only way
# to discover them was to deploy and read the crash, which is the worst possible moment.
#
# They are not formalities. PER in loop 1000A is who a payer telephones when a claim is unreadable,
# and N3/N4 in loop 2010AA is the provider's SERVICE address, which is how the plan's network and
# jurisdiction are decided. A clearinghouse rejects an 837 without them ("Segment PER has fewer
# than the minimum of 1 items"; "Unexpected segment REF: segment N3 is the next required segment").
#
# A PO box is not acceptable to most payers here — this must be where care is actually given.
BILLING_PROVIDER_PHONE=5105550100
BILLING_PROVIDER_STREET=1 SYNTHETIC TEST WAY
BILLING_PROVIDER_CITY=SPRINGFIELD
# Two-letter USPS code. Boot fails on anything else.
BILLING_PROVIDER_STATE=CA
# 5 or 9 digits; hyphens are stripped.
BILLING_PROVIDER_ZIP=90001
# The billing provider's Health Care Provider Taxonomy code, from NPPES — 837D loop 2000A PRV.
# Payers reject a claim without it when no rendering provider is reported, which is every solo
# practice's claim (Stedi edit 33). 1223G0001X = general practice dentistry. Required, with the
# address above, wherever a clearinghouse is wired; the demo identity supplies its own.
BILLING_PROVIDER_TAXONOMY=1223G0001X

# Set to 1 when this practice bills under a GROUP NPI rather than an individual one. It makes every
# claim require the treating dentist in 837D loop 2310B — without which the payer is told the
# practice itself rendered the care, and checks credentialing against the wrong entity. Stated, never
# inferred: an NPI does not carry its own entity type in a form this system can rely on.
# PRACTICE_IS_GROUP=0

# The practice's merchant discount rate in BASIS POINTS (295 = 2.95%), from its card processor's
# statement. Used only to price what a non-ACH payer reimbursement cost to receive: a payer paying by
# virtual credit card is charging the practice a percentage of money it already earned.
#
# Leave it unset unless the practice has actually stated its rate. A non-ACH payment is DETECTED and
# reported either way; without this, the observation says the cost is "not known" rather than
# reporting a plausible industry figure nobody measured — which a biller cannot defend to a payer.
# 0 is a valid statement (a processor that charges nothing); a malformed or out-of-range value is
# treated as unset, never rounded into something usable.
# MERCHANT_DISCOUNT_RATE_BPS=295

# --- Optional tuning ---
# How often the outbox worker drains, in milliseconds — in the web process AND `npm run worker`.
# Default 5000. Each drain is a query, so this is also how often the database is woken: a Neon
# compute only suspends after 5 idle minutes, and a 5s poll keeps it awake around the clock, which
# spends a free project's monthly compute in about two weeks. Above 300000 lets it sleep, at the cost
# of events waiting up to that long. Whole milliseconds from 1000; anything else refuses to boot.
# OUTBOX_INTERVAL_MS=5000
# This practice's participation in each payer's network, as `slug:in,slug:out`. Unset means every
# payer is `unknown`, which reads the 271 exactly as it was read before this existed — and `unknown`
# is a real answer, not a placeholder for in-network. An unparseable entry is IGNORED rather than
# defaulted: a typo must not silently move a payer's quotes to the other column.
# PAYER_NETWORK_PARTICIPATION=delta-dental:in,cigna-dental:out
